[0m$ [0mcd /home/agent/projects/netscript/wave7-billing-steer2 && git commit -F - <<'EOF'
Fix: every screen was rendering in a fallback font

`--ns-font-sans` named "Inter". `_app.tsx` requests DM Sans. Neither file
is wrong on its own, which is why this survived eleven passes that all read
both: the browser asks for Inter, does not have it, walks the stack to
`system-ui`, and renders the entire product in whatever the host happens to
ship. No error, no failing test, no visible symptom except that nothing
looks designed.

Two changes, and the rule that keeps the second one honest:

1. The token layer names the face the document loads. `--ns-font-sans` is
   DM Sans; every screen that was inheriting a system fallback now renders
   in the typeface the project already pays to download.

2. `--ns-font-display` is new, and it is the product's voice. A warm
   old-style serif (Fraunces, degrading to Georgia) spent only where a
   heading is read as a sentence: the wordmark, page and section headings,
   and the empty-state line that exists to be read. It never touches a
   figure — card titles, table headers, badges and every money span opt
   back out to the sans and numeric faces, because a date range or an
   amount set in a proportional serif is a trust problem, not a style.

The webfonts are now two `<link>` elements rather than one. The display
face and the UI face are different risks: a bad axis spec from the font
CDN on a combined request costs both and degrades silently to `system-ui`.
Split, a failure costs the voice and keeps the product.

`F1` in the design contract test makes this mechanical: every family the
token layer names must be a family `_app.tsx` requests, and the display
face must end in a generic serif. Verified non-vacuous — at the old value
it fails with `--ns-font-sans names "Inter", which _app.tsx never
requests`.
EOF
